{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## RCA — census, confirmed mechanism, and the design decision\n\n### 1. Census: how many keys are actually dropped TODAY — **2** (this one is LIVE, not latent)\n\n1979's honest headline was zero (its two `pop` sites each had a sibling `.get`). This one is\ndifferent. Method: an AST census over all **557** `src/rebar` `*.py` files for the exact\n`KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS` shape — a function whose `os.environ` key expression **references one of its\nown parameters** (the key only exists at the call site). That shape is sparse and enumerable:\n**9 functions in the whole tree**, 6 registered, 3 not.\n\n| unregistered shim | literal call sites | in `docs/env-vars.md`? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `_gate_str_pref` (`src/rebar/_config_resolvers.py:297`) | `\"REBAR_GATE_REF\"`, `\"REBAR_GATE_SOURCE\"` | **NO — both dropped** |\n| `read_secret_env` (`src/rebar/config.py:573`) | `\"JIRA_API_TOKEN\"` | yes — rescued by sibling direct reads (`runtime.py:307`, `acli_subprocess.py:119`) |\n| `_env_truthy` (`src/rebar/llm/config.py:323`) | none (uncalled in `src/rebar`) | n/a |\n\n**Measured: 2 real, operator-settable env vars are undocumented right now** —\n`REBAR_GATE_REF` and `REBAR_GATE_SOURCE`. Both are advertised to users in CLI help\n(`src/rebar/_cli/_parsers/advanced/llm.py:30,35`). The other two shims are latent in the\n1979 sense (a sibling read or no call site covers them).\n\n### 2. Confirmed mechanism (dynamic hypothesis, proven by execution — not by reading source)\n\nDiscriminating experiment: load the generator twice, add **only** the `_gate_str_pref` row to\nthe second, scan the real tree, diff.\n\n```\nBEFORE keys: 120   REBAR_GATE_REF present=False   REBAR_GATE_SOURCE present=False\nAFTER  keys: 122   REBAR_GATE_REF present=True    REBAR_GATE_SOURCE present=True\ndelta: ['REBAR_GATE_REF', 'REBAR_GATE_SOURCE']\npython scripts/gen_env_registry.py --check  ->  exit 0   (before AND after: the silence)\n```\n\nThe delta is *exactly* those two keys, which rules out the alternative \"they are missing for some\nother reason\" (filtered by the alias/MCP union, removed pre-1.0, or shadowed). 120/122 are raw\n`scan()` keys; the committed doc's 162 is post alias/MCP-union.\n\nThe mechanism, precisely: `_gate_str_pref` does `os.environ.get(env_name)`. The scanner sees a\nkey-bearing read with a **non-literal** key and files it in the *dynamic* list\n(`docs/env-vars.md` currently lists `src/rebar/_config_resolvers.py:305`). The literals live at\nthe **call sites**, one level up, and are only resolved if the callee name is in\n`KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS`. It is not, so they are walked past. **The dynamic list is the tell and also\nthe camouflage**: a registered helper's internal read appears there identically to an\nunregistered one's, so the artifact cannot distinguish \"keys resolved upstream\" from \"keys lost\".\n\n### 3. Defect legitimacy: `defect`, with an in-tree cited contract\n\n`src/rebar/_config_resolvers.py:288-289` asserts, in the seam header directly above the\noffending helper:\n\n> \"This is the composition seam the config-ownership gate treats as OWNED, so the env-name\n> literals **stay visible to `gen_env_registry`** here.\"\n\nThey do not. That is an explicit, authored, in-tree statement of intended behaviour that the\ncurrent tree violates — independent of this ticket's own ACs and of the reporter's expectation.\nCorroborating: `b00f-c62e-d3b9-4e63` closed the same class as a `Fixed:` bug.\n\n### 4. Defect origin — `caused_by`\n\nTwo candidates were separated with line-scoped blame plus a date test.\n\n**Recorded origin: `0f21-ca03-f063-4562` (piscine-unanimated-astarte)** — `c1f17861ec`,\n2026-07-08, \"generated REBAR_* env-var registry with a CI drift gate\". It introduced\n`KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS` as a name-keyed **fail-open** allowlist; `git blame -w -C -M -L 107,121`\nlands 11 of 15 dict lines there. Scope must match cause: this ticket's defect is the *design\nproperty*, which that commit created whole and which has never been closed. It is also the\nprecedent — sibling bug `739f` (blocky-matricidal-squid) attributed the other fail-open axis to\nthe same ticket on identical evidence.\n\n**Blame divergence, stated explicitly (per the AGENTS.md/attribution rule).** A *whole-file*\nblame on `scripts/gen_env_registry.py` names `9b1d2e6808` (ticket `739f`) as both the largest\ncontributor (173 lines vs 169) and the most recent toucher. That is the wrong answer here:\n`9b1d2e6808` rewrote the `os.environ` **accessor** recogniser and deliberately left the helper\ntable alone — it is the sibling fix, not the cause. Line-scoped blame on the\n`KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS` block is the correct instrument. Same shape as 1979's own rejection of its\nwhole-file default.\n\n**Second finding worth recording: the 2 dropped keys are a REGRESSION with a different origin.**\n`REBAR_GATE_REF`/`REBAR_GATE_SOURCE` were **documented from 2026-07-08 through 2026-08-18** and\ndropped to zero at `269b4d5678` (\"RP-04 C3d: cut llm-subsystem reads to config seam\", ticket\n`1b07-bf4d-4aca-41c4`, cookable-governing-cockatoo), which deleted `def _str_pref` — **a live\n`KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS` row** — and introduced `_gate_str_pref` in its place without renaming the\nrow. That commit regenerated and committed `docs/env-vars.md` in the same change, so a diff\nshowing two deleted rows was on screen and `--check` stayed green. `1b07` is the correct\n`caused_by` for the *instance*; `0f21` for the *class*, which is this ticket. Recording both so\nthe record is not fragmented.\n\n### 5. Side-finding: the table is stale in the OTHER direction too\n\nFour of the eleven current rows — `_rebar_env`, `_env_int`, `_str_pref`, `_int_pref` — have **no\n`def` site under `src/rebar` at all**; the RP-04 cutover drained them (`269b4d5678`,\n`d361eb28e4`). They are harmless (they match nothing) and are a *different* defect from this\none — nothing validates a row against the tree. **Not fixed in this change** (outside the proven\nroot cause); filed separately and linked `discovered_from`.\n\n### 6. Design decision — the symmetric answer DOES transfer, once the trigger is chosen correctly\n\nThe brief asked me to check the cost before assuming 1979's shape transfers. It does, because\nthe open-endedness objection dissolves under the right predicate.\n\n- **Rejected — \"any function whose body contains an env read\"** (the ticket's sketch #2 as\n  worded, and the review's G6 finding agrees). That fires on **97** functions in the tree,\n  nearly all of which read a *fixed literal* inline and are already captured correctly. Pure\n  false-positive engine; collides with AC#3.\n- **Rejected — a decorator or naming convention.** Invents a project-wide contract that every\n  future helper author must learn, and cannot see a helper written before the convention.\n- **Adopted — the parameterized-shim predicate:** *a function whose `os.environ` key expression\n  references one of its own parameters is an env-read helper by construction* — its keys live at\n  its call sites and are unreachable without a row. This is **not** open-ended: it is as closed\n  and enumerable as the `os.environ` attribute vocabulary 1979 fixed. **9 hits across 557\n  files (1.6%)**, and the remediation is one table row, exactly like adding an attribute to\n  `KEY_BEARING_ENVIRON_ATTRS`. It would have caught b00f (`_llm_float`), it catches\n  `_gate_str_pref` today, and it is position-agnostic (it does not care that `_llm_*` keeps its\n  env name at index 2). So the generator is not made obstructive: it fires roughly once per\n  new *shim*, which the project produces about nine times in its life.\n\n**Why not simply reuse `check_config_ownership.py`'s `_SHIM_RE` prior art** (review finding T1 —\na real and useful pointer). That detector is a **call-site name heuristic**:\n`^_.*(env|pref|getenv)` plus an env-name-shaped literal at **argument 0**. It is complementary,\nnot sufficient, for registry completeness: it misses `read_secret_env` (no leading underscore),\nmisses the whole `_llm_*` family (literal at index 2, not 0), keys on spelling rather than\nbehaviour, and lives in a gate whose seam-exception machinery deliberately tolerates its own\n\"unresolved-env-read\" findings inside owned composition seams — which is precisely why\n`_gate_str_pref` sits in an OWNED seam and that gate exits 0 while the registry is incomplete.\nThe predicate above keys on the dataflow instead, so it is a strict superset for this hazard.\nBoth stay; neither is deleted.\n\n### 7. What this deliberately does NOT sweep in (following 1979's precedent, not new boundaries)\n\nUnchanged and explicitly out of scope, exactly as 1979 documented them: reads under `tests/`\n(outside the scan root by design — note `tests/external/conftest.py` has its own `_env_truthy`\ntwin and stays untouched); **non-literal keys** at a call site (still reported as `dynamic`, not\ndropped); **`getattr` / string indirection**, which no static scan can resolve; and **`os.environ`\npassed by reference** into another callable. Two further exclusions specific to this predicate,\nstated so the fail-closed language does not over-claim: a key **composed** from a parameter\nwithout referencing it as a whole is still covered (the predicate is \"references a parameter\",\nnot \"is a parameter\"), but a key derived from a **regex match or other runtime source** rather\nthan a parameter is not a shim and does not fire — e.g. `llm/workflow`'s `sub_env`/`_resolve_scoped`\nread `os.environ.get(m.group(1))`, which is genuinely dynamic and correctly stays in the dynamic\nlist. And **stale rows** (§5) are a different defect, not covered.\n\n### 8. Consumer / caller-dependency sweep (review finding G1G2)\n\n`scripts/check_config_ownership.py:33-38` imports `scan`, `KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS`, `_is_os_environ`,\n`_str_literal` directly, and its `check()` calls `_check_registry_completeness()` →\n`derive_env_baseline()` → `scan(src/rebar)` **on the real tree** — so every one of the ~40\nheld-out tests in `tests/scripts/test_check_config_ownership_heldout.py` transitively scans\n`src/rebar`. This is exactly the repo-wide oracle that bit 1979 twice; it is in the gate list\nbelow, not left to CI. Two concrete interactions, both handled:\n\n- `scan()`'s **2-tuple arity is preserved** (`check_config_ownership.py:164` unpacks it). The\n  fail-closed signal is an exception, never a third element — same contract 1979 set.\n- Adding a `_gate_str_pref` row **reclassifies** it in that gate from `_shim_access`\n  (\"unresolved-env-read\") to `_helper_access` (a resolved env-read). That is the intended\n  direction, and `test_shim_shaped_absent_helper_fails_closed` uses `_mystery_env`, a name that\n  is not being registered, so the held-out contract is unaffected.\n\n### 9. Re: the review's blocking finding (E4/A1) — acknowledged, and it changes the test design\n\nThe reviewer is right that `tests/unit/test_env_registry_helper_coverage.py` already carries a\ndurable guard, and that a naively-written new test would pass today. Its guard is scoped to\n**`_llm_*` resolvers taking an `env_name` parameter in `llm/config.py`** — which is exactly why\nit did not catch `_gate_str_pref` in `_config_resolvers.py`. The deliverable therefore\n**generalises that guard from the `_llm_*` family to the whole parameterized-shim predicate**\nrather than adding a parallel test, and the new RED oracle targets a shape that escapes **both**\nexisting guards: not an `_llm_*`-named function in `config.py`, and not a `_SHIM_RE`-matching\nname. The b00f pins stay where they are; they are not duplicated.\n\n### 10. Gates to be run (not delegated to CI)\n\n`make lint` (incl. the shrink-only complexity ratchet: `new=0 increased=0 stale=0`),\n`make typecheck`, the **full unit sweep**, `tests/scripts/test_check_config_ownership_heldout.py`,\n`python scripts/check_config_ownership.py`, and `python scripts/gen_env_registry.py --check`\nafter regenerating. `docs/env-vars.md` **will** change (162 → 164) and is regenerated in the same\ncommit — a generator fix leaving a stale artifact is half a fix."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787299939818633001,"uuid":"9645b1ea-4216-48b3-8422-7f2661f9d2b5"}